## The First Circulation

Inside the fetus, even the circulation tells the same story.

The fetal lungs are not yet functioning as the organs of oxygen exchange they will become after birth. Oxygen arrives through the placenta.

The cardiovascular system therefore operates according to a temporary design suited to an intrauterine life.

Oxygenated blood returns from the placenta through the umbilical vein. Special routing allows a substantial portion of this blood to bypass much of the liver through the ductus venosus. It then enters the heart and is preferentially directed across the foramen ovale toward the left side of the circulation and the ascending aorta.

The developing brain and heart consequently receive relatively well-oxygenated blood.

Blood returning from the upper body follows another route. Because the fetal lungs are fluid-filled and pulmonary vascular resistance remains high, much of the blood entering the pulmonary artery is diverted through the ductus arteriosus into the descending aorta.

Three temporary shunts—ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus—allow the fetal circulation to function without pulmonary respiration.

Then birth arrives.

The placenta is no longer the organ of oxygen exchange.

The lungs expand.

Pulmonary vascular resistance falls.

Pressure relationships change.

The fetal shunts begin to close.

The circulatory system reorganizes itself around a new world.

Birth is therefore not a reset button.

It is a transition between two biological environments.

The organism that emerges at birth has not suddenly become “developed.” It has changed systems.

