## Building a Brain

Few biological processes illustrate developmental timing more vividly than the construction of the human brain.

The cerebral cortex does not appear as a finished structure that merely grows larger.

It is constructed.

Neural progenitor cells proliferate.

New neurons are generated.

Cells migrate.

Neurons differentiate.

Axons extend.

Connections form.

Some cells die through programmed developmental processes.

Networks are refined.

The developing cerebral cortex emerges from the rostral neural tube. Neurons generated in ventricular and subventricular zones migrate outward, many travelling along radial glial scaffolds. The cortical plate develops through an ordered sequence of cellular arrivals, producing layers and temporary compartments that guide subsequent connectivity.

Among these transient structures is the subplate, an important staging environment through which early connections, including thalamocortical pathways, are organized before mature cortical circuitry is established.

This is not merely a beautiful biological detail.

It changes the way we should speak about prenatal brain development.

The fetal brain is not a smaller version of the adult brain.

It is a brain under construction.

That construction is extraordinarily sophisticated, but it is unfinished by design.

Neural development continues after birth. Experience does not arrive after the brain has completed its wiring. Experience becomes part of the continuing process by which circuits are strengthened, reorganized, and functionally integrated.

This is why prenatal development matters without determining everything that follows.

Early development establishes structures and constraints.

Later experience continues the construction.

