## When Development Goes Wrong—and Why Timing Still Matters

A developing system can be vulnerable because development is active.

But vulnerability must be described with precision.

Some prenatal disturbances have severe and well-established consequences. Certain infections can disrupt brain development. Severe disturbances of oxygenation or growth can compromise developing tissues. Selected toxic exposures can produce structural or neurodevelopmental effects. Major nutritional deficiencies can interfere with processes that require particular biochemical substrates.

But these examples should not be converted into a universal narrative of prenatal fragility.

The same biological system that can be vulnerable is also capable of regulation and adaptation.

Nor are all outcomes equivalent.

A change in cortical surface area is not the same thing as an alteration in cognition.

An altered fetal heart-rate measure is not the same thing as psychiatric illness.

A difference in birth weight is not a measure of intelligence.

A biomarker is not a personality.

The further downstream the claim, the more variables enter the system.

Genes matter.

Placental function matters.

Maternal health matters.

Gestational timing matters.

Postnatal nutrition matters.

Caregiving matters.

Education matters.

Health care matters.

Social conditions matter.

Development is cumulative, but it is not mechanically additive.

