## Nutrition Is Construction Material

A developing human body cannot be built without raw materials.

The fetus requires oxygen, glucose, amino acids, fatty acids, vitamins, minerals, and other substrates. The placenta regulates their movement and availability. Maternal metabolic state affects circulating concentrations. Placental transport systems respond to developmental demand and physiological conditions.

This makes pregnancy nutrition important.

It does not make every nutritional claim true.

Some interventions have strong evidence for specific outcomes.

Folic acid is the classic example. Periconceptional folic acid substantially reduces the risk of neural-tube defects. The evidence is strong because the intervention, timing, biological mechanism, and outcome align.

That is what a useful causal story looks like.

The intervention is defined.

The window is defined.

The outcome is defined.

The effect can be measured.

And the evidence does not require us to claim that folic acid determines general intelligence or personality.

Other nutrients are more complicated.

Iron matters.

Iodine matters.

Calcium matters.

Vitamin D, B12, choline and other nutrients have biologically plausible roles and varying levels of evidence depending on the population, baseline nutritional status, intervention, dose, and outcome.

This distinction matters because pregnancy has become surrounded by a commercial culture of optimization.

The scientific message is usually much simpler.

Correct important deficiencies.

Provide evidence-based antenatal care.

Ensure access to adequate food and essential nutrients.

Do not turn every uncertainty into another supplement.

