## Iodine and the Problem of Timing

Iodine offers one of the clearest examples of why developmental biology cannot be separated from time.

Thyroid hormones participate in processes essential to brain development, including neuronal differentiation, migration, and maturation. During early pregnancy, fetal thyroid function is not yet fully established, making maternal thyroid hormone availability particularly important.

Severe iodine deficiency can produce devastating developmental consequences.

That fact is well established.

But the correct lesson is not that a parent must maintain perfect nutritional control over every day of pregnancy.

The lesson is more precise:

**some biological substrates become especially important at particular developmental moments.**

This is precisely why public-health interventions are valuable.

Population-level nutritional programs can prevent severe deficiency without requiring parents to become amateur developmental biologists.

The distinction between prevention and optimization matters.

Prevention aims to avoid a defined harm.

Optimization often implies that every variable can be pushed toward an ideal.

Human development does not offer that kind of precision.

