## Fetal Growth Is a Measure, Not a Destiny

Eventually the developmental system becomes visible.

Growth can be measured.

Fetal size, growth velocity, head circumference, placental function, and birth weight offer concrete indicators of what has happened during pregnancy.

But measurement does not eliminate interpretation.

Fetal growth reflects multiple influences at once: genetic characteristics, placental function, maternal physiology, nutrition, gestational age, environmental conditions, and disease.

A small infant may be constitutionally small.

Another may be growth restricted because placental function is inadequate.

Two infants with the same birth weight can arrive there through very different biological pathways.

This is why fetal growth restriction is clinically meaningful while birth size itself should never be treated as a general measure of human potential.

Growth standards help clinicians identify unusual trajectories, but standards are not destiny either.

Some infants who experience growth restriction show substantial postnatal catch-up.

Others retain differences.

Some early differences become less pronounced.

Some change into different functional patterns.

Development has trajectories, not just starting points.

