## Plasticity

This is perhaps the most important idea in the chapter.

Development is plastic.

Plasticity does not mean unlimited flexibility.

It means that the developing organism can adapt, compensate, reorganize, and respond to changing conditions.

An early difference can persist.

It can attenuate.

It can be transformed.

Or we may simply not know.

The scientific error is to assume persistence because an early finding seems biologically important.

Suppose a fetal physiological measure differs from that of another fetus.

What happens next?

Does the difference remain at birth?

Does it remain at one year?

Does it remain at three?

Does it become functionally meaningful?

Does another system compensate?

Does the postnatal environment alter the trajectory?

These are empirical questions.

They cannot be answered by biological plausibility alone.

The same principle applies to adversity.

An exposure can matter without determining the future.

A risk factor can be causal without being destiny.

A biological effect can persist without producing disability.

A population can experience elevated risk while most individuals remain unaffected.

This is not a weakness in developmental science.

It is one of its central findings.

